  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NEXUS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(USAID Sahel Regional Program):  Nexus activity strengthens the resilience of public drinking water services, amid the insecurity crisis, in the main urban centers hosting Internally Displaced People (IDPs) in Burkina Faso. By improving the quality of drinking water services, the activity will benefit the total population of 113,967 people in Titao (Nord region) and Kongoussi (Centre-Nord region) municipalities, including 44,031 IDPs. .Since the start of 2019, Burkina Faso has faced a significant increase in humanitarian needs amid worsening insecurity in the Sahel, North, Center-North and East regions. This crisis has generated an exponential increase in the number of internally displaced persons (IDPs) fleeing violence. This has led to significantly more pressure being put on water infrastructure. Prior to the crisis, ensuring universal access to a continuous, sustainable, and affordable drinking water service was already a major challenge for the country with a significant production deficit more than tripled by the influx of displaced people.  Nexus supports the National Office of Water and Sanitation (ONEA) and municipalities in their role as authorities managing public drinking water services in crisis contexts, strengthens the performance of public drinking water utilities in a crisis context, and develops methodologies and disseminates lessons learned in order to facilitate the replication of the approach in other urban centers hosting IDPs. .This pilot activity is intended to scale innovations that will solidify sustained collaboration between USAID humanitarian and development activities in the Sahel, enhance the tool kit to manage the humanitarian-development nexus, and provide Agency-wide learning. It contributes to the Resilience in the Sahel Enhanced (RISE) II initiative. $244,647 of funding contributes to meeting the required wash earmark, and $50,000 of funding contributes meeting the required microenterprise earmark of $4,000,000. .</narrative>
  </description>
